{"task_id": "HumanEval/119", "solution": "```python\ndef match_parens(lst):\n    '''\n    You are given a list of two strings, both strings consist of open\n    parentheses '(' or close parentheses ')' only.\n    Your job is to check if it is possible to concatenate the two strings in\n    some order, that the resulting string will be good.\n    A string S is considered to be good if and only if all parentheses in S\n    are balanced. For example: the string '(())()' is good, while the string\n    '())' is not.\n    Return 'Yes' if there's a way to make a good string, and return 'No' otherwise.\n    '''\n\n    # Calculates the final balance and minimum balance encountered in a string.\n    def _check_balance(s: str) -> tuple[int, int]:\n        balance = 0\n        min_balance = 0\n        for char in s:\n            if char == '(':\n                balance += 1\n            else:\n                balance -= 1\n            min_balance = min(min_balance, balance)\n        return balance, min_balance\n\n    # Checks if concatenating s1 and s2 results in a balanced string.\n    # It verifies total balance is zero and no prefix falls below zero.\n    def can_form_balanced(s1: str, s2: str) -> bool:\n        bal1, min_bal1 = _check_balance(s1)\n        bal2, min_bal2 = _check_balance(s2)\n\n        if bal1 + bal2 != 0: # Total balance must be zero.\n            return False\n        if min_bal1 < 0: # No prefix of s1 should be negative.\n            return False\n        if bal1 + min_bal2 < 0: # The combined string must not drop below zero balance in s2 part.\n            return False\n\n        return True\n\n    s1, s2 = lst[0], lst[1]\n\n    # Check s1 + s2 concatenation.\n    if can_form_balanced(s1, s2):\n        return 'Yes'\n\n    # Check s2 + s1 concatenation.\n    if can_form_balanced(s2, s1):\n        return 'Yes'\n\n    return 'No'\n```"}
